ard and in hand, with 4♠ also present — that’s four spades (A, J, 4 from board + your J and 6). Wait — correction: you hold J♠ and 6♠, board has A♠, 4♠, and 9♥ → so three spades on board (A♠, 4♠, 9♥ is not spade — wait: 9♥ is hearts). Let's recheck:\n\nBoard:  \n- A♠ (Spades)  \n- 9♥ (Hearts)  \n- 4♠ (Spades)  \n\nSo two spades on board: A♠ and 4♠.  \nYou hold: J♠ and 6♠ → both spades.  \nTherefore, you have **four spades**: A♠, J♠, 6♠, 4♠ → four to a flush (Spades). One more spade on turn or river completes the flush.\n\nAdditionally, possible straights?  \nYour cards: J, 6. Board: A, 9, 4.  \nPossible connectors: 10 and Q would make Broadway straight (10-J-Q-K-A), but you don’t have that.  \nOr 8-9-10-J-Q → missing 8, 10, Q. Only 9 and J present. So gutshot straight draws are weak. Best hope is flush.\n\nYou currently have top pair (Jacks with A high board) but not even that — you have J-high with A on board, so actually **second pair** type holding? No — no pair yet. You have J♠ and 6♠, board has A, 9, 4 — so you don't have a pair. Just **high card Ace and Jack**, with four-to-flush draw.\n\nSo:  \n- You have a **flush draw** (9 outs: any spade completes flush).  \n- Also, backdoor straight potential, but very weak.  \n- Currently, you likely do not have a made hand (no pair), but strong draw.\n\nOpponent checked on flop (Round 1 action: 0). That suggests weakness or trap. Since they didn’t bet, they may have missed the flop too.\n\nYou can check behind and see next card for free, or bet to build pot/imply strength.\n\nBut since you’re out of position and drawing, betting might be risky unless semi-bluffing.\n\nSemi-bluff raise could work: represent strength (like Ax), make weaker hands fold.\n\nBut opponent may have Ax themselves (common), so bluffing may fail.\n\nBest play: low-risk continuation. Since opponent checked, you can **check back** and keep pot small, preserving chips while chasing draw.\n\nRaising 100 risks building a big pot out of position without