flop in most heads-up situations when you're out of position, but since no bet has been made yet, checking could be used to see the flop cheaply. However, as the button or aggressor in heads-up play, raising is often preferred with hands like Q7o to take initiative.\n- Raising to 200 (or higher) asserts aggression, builds the pot when you’re likely ahead of a wide range, and can force weaker hands to fold. Given that you are short on chips compared to starting stack and slightly trailing, applying pressure early with a standard raise is reasonable.\n- Going all-in (-2) pre-flop with Q7o is overly aggressive and risky. It commits your stack with a hand that is dominated by stronger queens or any pair, putting you at risk of being exploited.\n\nReason:  \nQ7o is not a strong hand, but it's playable in a heads-up context, especially if you're on the button. Since no community cards are out, this is pre-flop. A standard raise to 200 (assuming big blind is 100) represents a 2x minimum raise, which is typical. This action helps define your opponent’s range, maintains aggression, and avoids over-committing. It balances value and bluff equity while keeping options open post-flop.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
