ight (9-J would give 8-9-10-J-Q). You're missing one card in the middle — specifically, a 9 or a Jack. There are 8 outs for this (four 9s and four Jacks).\n- **Flush Draw**: You have two diamonds in hand (Q♦), and there are two diamonds on the board (10♦, 5♦), so you have four cards to a flush. That gives you **9 outs** (13 total diamonds minus 4 seen = 9 remaining diamonds).\n- However, note that some overlap exists: if a **9♦** or **J♦** comes, it helps both draws, but we must avoid double-counting. Total unique outs ≈ **15** (since 9♦ and J♦ are shared).\n\nWith ~15 outs, your chance of improving by the river is significant (~30–35% combined chance of hitting flush or straight). This is a strong draw.\n\nPosition-wise, you raised pre-flop (200), and your opponent called (action: 0). That suggests they may have a decent but not necessarily premium hand. They could have a middle/bottom pair, overcards, or a weaker draw.\n\nCurrently, it's the **flop**, and no bet has been made yet in this round. You have the initiative from your pre-flop raise. Checking gives up aggression and lets your opponent see free cards, possibly outdrawing you. Betting or raising can build the pot while you’re ahead in equity (as a draw) or take it down immediately.\n\nYour valid actions include checking (0), folding (-1), calling isn’t applicable since no bet was made, or raising at least 100 (which seems to be the minimum increment here — possibly the big blind).\n\nBut note: Since no one has bet post-flop, **you should consider continuation betting (c-bet)** to represent strength and capitalize on fold equity.\n\nRaising around **600–800** would be standard relative to pot size (pot is currently 400: your 200 + opponent’s 200 from pre-flop), so a raise to ~600–800 makes sense. But the system allows any value ≥100.\n\nHowever, the options seem constrained in format — you can choose **any amount above 100**, but smaller raises may look weak.\n\nGoing all-in now (-2) is too premature with such a strong draw — you’d price yourself out of bluffing later and force only better hands or correct calls.\n\nFolding (-1) is terrible — you have massive equity and fold equity.\n\nChecking (0) risks giving a free card — especially dangerous since opponent might hit a worse draw or pair.\n\nBest move: **Raise moderately** to charge opponents’ draws and build the pot while representing strength.\n\nGiven your chip stack (19,750), even a 600–800 raise is safe.\n\nBut the interface appears to expect a **numerical value**, and minimal raise is 100. To be effective, you must raise more than just 100.\n\nA raise of **600** is reasonable — about 1.5x the pot (pot is 400), which is a standard c-bet sizing with a draw.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a very strong draw (15 outs) and the initiative. A moderate raise builds the pot, protects your hand, and applies pressure. It can make weaker pairs or draws fold, winning the pot now, or get called by worse hands. It also sets you up for further action on the turn.\n\nAnswer: 600"}
